# Weekly /retro (fleet-ops#9314), rebuilt so the four blockers of the review on
# PR #9321 are closed by structure, not by prompt text (fleet-ops#9641, #9339).
#
# What runs: Monday 07:00 IST (retro-weekly.timer) one headless Claude Code call
# reads a week of fleet sessions and writes a retro report. Four things hold
# whatever the model reads or is told by text inside the sessions:
#
# 1. No admin login. The model runs inside a bwrap jail built from the worker
#    jail of agent.yml (empty tmpfs over the gh and Cloudflare config) but
#    stricter: /home is an empty tmpfs and only what the retro reads is bound
#    back, read-only. Not bound, so not readable: the gh login, the Cloudflare
#    tokens, fleet-ops seat files, the worker App key, ssh keys, the other
#    Claude logins, every .env. The runtime directory (where the issue token
#    lives) is hidden too. The Claude config and the vault are read-only, so a
#    fooled model cannot plant a hook, a setting or a shim that later runs
#    outside the jail. The jailed process has NO GitHub token at all. The one
#    token the run uses is the worker App's installation token limited to
#    issues:write on this one repo (fleet-grafana-github-token.service mints it
#    every 20 minutes; this unit only reads it). It is used by the filing step
#    below, outside the jail, with an empty GH_CONFIG_DIR so the gh login is
#    never read (fleet-ops#9339 asked for exactly this scope; the App token
#    gives it without a hand-minted file). Scope, from the mint request in
#    fleet-grafana-github-token.service ("repositories":["fleet-ops"],
#    "permissions":{"issues":"write"}) and probed 2026-10-12: the token lists one
#    repository, fleet-ops, and gets 403 on that repo's Actions secrets and on its
#    self-hosted runners.
# 2. Private reports. The report is a file in the vault (_system/retro/, which
#    syncs to Nish's Mac and never reaches a public repo). Follow-up work is
#    filed as an issue only in a fixed form: the model writes a five-line file
#    (a class, a count, a fix rung, an owning repo and a fix kind, each from a
#    list, the count a number), and the filing step builds the
#    public title and body from a template. No free text, quote, id, path or
#    time can reach the page, because the grammar has nowhere to put them. The
#    built text then also goes through config/retro-public-deny.txt (a session
#    id, a /home or other path, a quoted line, a time of day, a link, an email or
#    a secret shape): one refusal fails the unit and nothing is filed. The model
#    cannot choose labels, repo or the token: it only writes files, and a
#    different process files them. The only label is retro: needs-orchestrator
#    would start a paid orchestrator run per issue (agent-dispatch.yml). The title
#    is fixed per class and a class that already has an open issue is skipped.
#    The report is also scrubbed of secret shapes before it reaches the vault.
# 3. Approved provider only. The call is Claude Code to the Anthropic API, the
#    provider that already holds these sessions, with the model pinned below.
#    It does not use the router. The `ollama` seat is NOT local: its rows are
#    api_base https://ollama.com (config/litellm-proxy.yaml) and nothing
#    listens on this host's Ollama port. A step after the run reads the call's
#    own record and fails the unit unless the main model is the pinned one and
#    every model used is a claude-* model; it prints the pinned provider to the
#    journal. ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL is pinned and checked against a literal before
#    the model starts, user settings are not loaded (--setting-sources), and
#    the other provider switches are unset.
# 4. Live only after review. Nothing ships on the host until this file is on
#    main; fleet-sync.service then links it (step 2e), and the timer is enabled
#    by hand once (README "Weekly retro").
#
# Network: the jail has none. bwrap --unshare-net leaves only loopback; the one
# way out is retro-weekly-api.socket, bound into the jail, which systemd serves
# with systemd-socket-proxyd to api.anthropic.com:443 (retro-weekly-api.service).
# A stock socat inside the jail joins the model's loopback port to that socket,
# and /etc/hosts inside maps the API name to the loopback port. TLS stays end to
# end with the real host name, so the destination is fixed by the proxy's own
# address and cannot be changed from inside. A request to any other host from
# inside the jail fails.
# Tools: the permission file (config/retro-weekly-claude.json) allows reading,
# searching and a short list of read-only commands, denies the network tools and
# anything that can exec (find -exec, rg --pre), and the mode is dontAsk so a
# call outside the list is refused, not asked. That list is the second fence, not
# the first: a command that slips past it still has no network, a read-only
# system and one writable directory.
#
# The network door has three small parts, each with one reason. A stock socat
# inside the jail joins the CLI's TCP to the filesystem socket, the only thing a
# network-less jail can reach; the CLI keeps the real API host name (an /etc/hosts
# line inside maps it to loopback) so TLS verifies against the true host, on an
# unprivileged port because the jail has no capabilities. The systemd-native
# form (PrivateNetwork=yes on this service, and on the socket unit
# ListenStream=127.0.0.1:8443 with PrivateNetwork=yes and JoinsNamespaceOf= this
# service) was tried on this host's user manager (2026-10-12) and fails: the
# socket unit logs "Failed to join network namespace: Operation not permitted"
# and "Failed to listen on sockets", and the dependent service does not start.
# PrivateNetwork=yes alone works for a service, but a user manager cannot place a
# socket in it. Claude
# Code's own sandbox allow-list fences the commands the CLI runs, not the CLI, and
# would need nested namespaces inside this jail; it can be a later extra layer.
# The one-second wait for socat is not a race that can hurt: the CLI retries a
# refused first connection.
#
# Why bwrap and not the unit's own sandbox directives (PrivateNetwork=,
# TemporaryFileSystem=): the jail is the worker jail of agent.yml on purpose, so
# there is one jail design to audit and the review of #9321 asked for exactly
# that pattern. Why a deny list over built text and not only the harness's
# --json-schema: a schema is enforced inside the process the model steers; the
# check here runs outside it, on the bytes that would be published, and fails the
# unit by itself.
#
# The managed policy settings under /etc/claude-code are root-installed and apply
# to this call too; checked 2026-10-12: they carry no allow rule, so they add
# no command to the permission file, only deny and ask rules and one hook. The Claude login
# file is within the model's reach (the call needs it) but it has no way out: no
# network but the API host, a report scrubbed of token shapes, and public issues
# made only of listed values.
#
# A token refresh cannot happen in the jail: the only host reachable is the API
# host, and the refresh goes to another one, so the shared login is never
# rotated by a retro run (and ~/.claude is read-only anyway).
#
# Gates, each a failed unit someone can list (OnFailure= pages): the pin check,
# a non-empty report, the public-text check, and ExecStopPost looking for the
# vault copy. `claude -p` exits 0 on a model refusal, so the success of the call
# is read from its own result record, not its exit status.
#
# One date: ExecStartPre writes today's IST date once into the runtime
# directory; every later step reads it, so a catch-up run that crosses midnight
# still names the report and its gate with the same string.
#
# %%, $$: systemd expands % and $ itself before any shell runs, so every shell
# $ is written $$ and every shell % is written %% (ci.yml gate fleet-ops#8382).
# The shell lines use bash, single-quoted, with no single quote inside.
#
# Switch off: systemctl --user disable --now retro-weekly.timer
# Delete: the README "Weekly retro" section lists the files.
[Unit]
Description=Weekly /retro over one week of fleet sessions (Monday 07:00 IST)
After=fleet-grafana-github-token.service retro-weekly-api.socket
Requires=retro-weekly-api.socket
OnFailure=fleet-unit-failed@%N.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
RuntimeDirectory=retro-weekly
RuntimeDirectoryMode=0700
WorkingDirectory=%h
Environment=FLEET_TASK_ID=retro-weekly
Environment=TZ=Asia/Kolkata
Environment=LC_ALL=C.UTF-8
Environment=RETRO_STATE=%h/workspaces/agent-state/retro
Environment=RETRO_VAULT=%h/workspaces/tooling/nish-vault/_system/retro
Environment=RETRO_REPO=%h/workspaces/tooling/fleet-ops-deploy-clone
Environment=RETRO_TOKEN=%t/fleet-grafana-github-token/token
Environment=RETRO_API_SOCK=%t/retro-weekly-api.sock
# Pinned provider and model. The pin check below compares against the literals
# in the script too, so a drop-in that changes only these lines fails the unit.
Environment=ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com
Environment=RETRO_MODEL=claude-sonnet-5-5
UnsetEnvironment=ANTHROPIC_AUTH_TOKEN ANTHROPIC_API_KEY ANTHROPIC_MODEL ANTHROPIC_SMALL_FAST_MODEL CLAUDE_CODE_USE_BEDROCK CLAUDE_CODE_USE_VERTEX CLAUDE_CODE_USE_FOUNDRY CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR GH_TOKEN GITHUB_TOKEN
# 1. Fresh run directory, the one date, the inputs that must exist, and the pin
#    check on the environment. Then the list of issues already filed (titles
#    only), fetched here with the issues token because the jailed model has none.
ExecStartPre=/bin/bash -c 'set -eu; d=$$(date +%%F); run="$$RETRO_STATE/run-$$d"; rm -rf "$$run"; mkdir -p "$$run/issues"; printf %%s "$$d" > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/date"; for f in prompts/retro-weekly.md config/retro-public-deny.txt config/retro-weekly-claude.json; do test -s "$$RETRO_REPO/$$f" || { echo "retro: missing $$f" >&2; exit 1; }; done; test "$$ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL" = https://api.anthropic.com && test "$$RETRO_MODEL" = claude-sonnet-5-5 || { echo "retro: provider or model pin changed, refusing to run" >&2; exit 1; }; test -s "$$RETRO_TOKEN" || { echo "retro: issues token missing" >&2; exit 1; }; mkdir -p "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/gh"; GH_CONFIG_DIR="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/gh" GH_TOKEN=$$(cat "$$RETRO_TOKEN") %h/.local/bin/gh issue list -R Nishfleet/fleet-ops --label retro --state open --limit 100 --json title -q ".[].title" > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/known-issues.txt"'
# 2. The model, in the jail. Stdin is the prompt, then the dynamic run header: the
#    week's start date and the session counts are worked out here, outside the jail,
#    because the jail's command list refuses the cd and the pipe to awk a model
#    reaches for to count them, and a run that gives up after a refusal writes no
#    report. The jail: system read-only, /home an empty tmpfs, then only these bound back.
#    --tmpfs over the gh and Cloudflare config is the worker jail's own pair
#    (agent.yml); under the empty /home they are redundant and stay so the line
#    reads the same as the worker jail. A copy of ~/.claude.json is bound so the
#    model cannot edit the real one, and ~/.claude is read-only including the
#    login file: a run that needs a token refresh fails loudly rather than let a
#    fooled model rewrite the login. Of the deploy clone only the permission
#    file is bound. /srv, /opt and the two system config dirs that hold service
#    env files and a token are emptied too (the system tree is otherwise
#    read-only and readable).
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'set -eu; umask 077; d=$$(cat "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/date"); run="$$RETRO_STATE/run-$$d"; cp "$$HOME/.claude.json" "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/claude.json"; printf "127.0.0.1 localhost api.anthropic.com\n" > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/hosts"; hide=(); for b in curl wget nc.openbsd openssl ssh python3 perl; do if [ -e /usr/bin/$$b ]; then hide+=(--ro-bind /dev/null /usr/bin/$$b); fi; done; for t in /srv /opt /var/lib/fleet-ops /etc/fail2ban; do if [ -d $$t ]; then hide+=(--tmpfs $$t); fi; done; { cat "$$RETRO_REPO/prompts/retro-weekly.md"; printf "RUN_DIR=%%s\nTODAY=%%s\nWEEK_START=%%s\nCLAUDE_SESSIONS=%%s\nPI_SESSIONS=%%s\n" "$$run" "$$d" "$$(date -d "$$d -7 days" +%%F)" "$$(find "$$HOME/.claude/projects" -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 2 -name "*.jsonl" -mtime -7 -printf "%%s\n" | jq -sr "[length, (add // 0)] | @tsv")" "$$(find "$$HOME/.pi/agent/sessions" -name "*.jsonl" -mtime -7 -printf "%%s\n" | jq -sr "[length, (add // 0)] | @tsv")"; } | bwrap --ro-bind / / --dev /dev --proc /proc --tmpfs /tmp --tmpfs /home --tmpfs /run "$${hide[@]}" --tmpfs "$$HOME/.config/gh" --tmpfs "$$HOME/.config/cloudflare" --ro-bind "$$HOME/.local/bin" "$$HOME/.local/bin" --ro-bind "$$HOME/.local/share/claude" "$$HOME/.local/share/claude" --ro-bind "$$HOME/.claude" "$$HOME/.claude" --bind "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/claude.json" "$$HOME/.claude.json" --ro-bind "$$HOME/.pi/agent/sessions" "$$HOME/.pi/agent/sessions" --ro-bind "$$HOME/AGENTS.md" "$$HOME/AGENTS.md" --ro-bind "$$RETRO_REPO/config/retro-weekly-claude.json" "$$RETRO_REPO/config/retro-weekly-claude.json" --ro-bind "$$HOME/workspaces/tooling/nish-vault/_system/shared-memory" "$$HOME/workspaces/tooling/nish-vault/_system/shared-memory" --bind "$$run" "$$run" --ro-bind "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/known-issues.txt" "$$run/known-issues.txt" --ro-bind "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/hosts" /etc/hosts --bind "$$RETRO_API_SOCK" /tmp/api.sock --chdir / --unshare-pid --unshare-net --die-with-parent -- bash -c "socat TCP-LISTEN:8443,bind=127.0.0.1,fork,reuseaddr UNIX-CONNECT:/tmp/api.sock & sleep 1; exec env ANTHROPIC_BASE_URL=https://api.anthropic.com:8443 CLAUDE_CODE_DISABLE_NONESSENTIAL_TRAFFIC=1 DISABLE_TELEMETRY=1 $$HOME/.local/bin/claude -p --model $$RETRO_MODEL --output-format stream-json --verbose --no-session-persistence --setting-sources project,local --settings $$RETRO_REPO/config/retro-weekly-claude.json --permission-mode dontAsk --max-turns 200" > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/claude.jsonl"'
# 3. Pin check: read the call's own record. It is written to the runtime directory,
#    which the jail hides, so the model cannot replace it with a forged one. Fails unless the call succeeded, the
#    main model is the pinned one and every model it touched is a claude-* model
#    (the Anthropic API). Prints the pinned provider; no transcript text.
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'set -eu; d=$$(cat "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/date"); run="$$RETRO_STATE/run-$$d"; jq -e -s \'([.[] | select(.type=="system" and .subtype=="init")] | length == 1 and .[0].model == env.RETRO_MODEL) and ([.[] | select(.type=="assistant" and (.parent_tool_use_id == null)) | .message.model] | length > 0 and all(. == env.RETRO_MODEL or . == "<synthetic>")) and ([.[] | select(.type=="result")] | length == 1 and .[0].is_error == false and .[0].subtype == "success" and (.[0].modelUsage | keys | all(test("^claude-"))))\' "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/claude.jsonl" > /dev/null || { echo "retro: PIN CHECK FAILED (wrong model, other provider, or the call did not succeed)" >&2; exit 1; }; used=$$(jq -r -s \'[.[] | select(.type=="result") | .modelUsage | keys[]] | unique | join(",")\' "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/claude.jsonl"); echo "retro-provider: anthropic host=api.anthropic.com:443 via the jail socket (the jail has no other network) pinned-model=$$RETRO_MODEL models-used=$$used auth=$$(jq -r -s \'.[] | select(.type=="system" and .subtype=="init") | .apiKeySource\' "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/claude.jsonl") router=none"'
# 4. The private report goes to the vault (syncs to the Mac; the vault repo is
#    private and ignores nothing under _system/retro).
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'set -eu; d=$$(cat "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/date"); run="$$RETRO_STATE/run-$$d"; test -f "$$run/report.md" && test ! -L "$$run/report.md" && test -s "$$run/report.md" || { echo "retro: no report written (or it is not a regular file)" >&2; exit 1; }; sed -E "s/gh[pousr]_[A-Za-z0-9]{20,}|github_pat_[A-Za-z0-9_]{20,}|sk-[A-Za-z0-9_-]{16,}|eyJ[A-Za-z0-9_-]{10,}|AKIA[0-9A-Z]{16}|[0-9]{8,10}:[A-Za-z0-9_-]{30,}|[A-Za-z0-9_-]{40,}|[A-Za-z0-9_]{32,}/[redacted]/g" "$$run/report.md" > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/report.md"; /usr/bin/install -D -m 0644 "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/report.md" "$$RETRO_VAULT/retro-week-$$d.md"; echo "retro: private report $$RETRO_VAULT/retro-week-$$d.md ($$(wc -c < "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/report.md") bytes, secret shapes redacted: $$(grep -o "[[]redacted[]]" "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/report.md" | wc -l))"'
# 5. File follow-up issues, only if every one passes the public-text check. All
#    files are checked before any is filed; one refusal fails the unit and files
#    nothing. Only line numbers are printed on a refusal, never the matched text.
#    The repo, labels and the token are fixed here, not chosen by the model. gh
#    reads an empty config dir plus the issues token, never the gh login.
ExecStart=/bin/bash -c 'set -eu; shopt -s nullglob; d=$$(cat "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/date"); run="$$RETRO_STATE/run-$$d"; deny="$$RETRO_REPO/config/retro-public-deny.txt"; test "$$(grep -c . "$$deny")" -ge 10 || { echo "retro-public-check: deny list is empty or short" >&2; exit 1; }; printf "%%s\n" "x 00000000-0000-0000-0000-000000000000 /home/x \\\"q\\\" 09:15" | grep -Eqif "$$deny" || { echo "retro-public-check: deny list failed its own canary" >&2; exit 1; }; if [ -L "$$run/issues" ]; then echo "retro-public-check: REFUSED, issues is a link" >&2; exit 1; fi; files=("$$run"/issues/*.md); n=$${#files[@]}; if [ "$$n" -gt 3 ]; then echo "retro-public-check: REFUSED, $$n issue files (max 3)" >&2; exit 1; fi; ok() { sed -n "$$1p" "$$3" | grep -Exq -- "$$2"; }; bad=0; : > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/queue"; : > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/seen"; for f in "$${files[@]}"; do b=$$(basename "$$f"); if [ -L "$$f" ] || [ ! -f "$$f" ] || [ "$$(wc -l < "$$f")" -ne 5 ] || [ "$$(wc -c < "$$f")" -gt 400 ]; then echo "retro-public-check: REFUSED $$b: not a regular five-line file" >&2; bad=1; continue; fi; rc=0; m=$$(grep -nEif "$$deny" "$$f") || rc=$$?; if [ "$$rc" -gt 1 ]; then echo "retro-public-check: grep failed on $$b" >&2; exit 1; fi; if [ "$$rc" -eq 0 ]; then echo "retro-public-check: REFUSED $$b as written by the model: session id, path, quote, time, link, email or secret shape on line(s) $$(printf "%%s\n" "$$m" | cut -d: -f1 | tr "\n" " ")" >&2; bad=1; continue; fi; ok 1 "class=(main-red|tool-economy|corrections|false-claims|repeated-failures|steering-no-ops)" "$$f" && ok 2 "count=[0-9]{1,5}" "$$f" && ok 3 "rung=(structure|static-gate|rule|skill|prose)" "$$f" && ok 4 "repo=(fleet-ops|0509|drive|inish-site|tinystudio-in)" "$$f" && ok 5 "fix=(ci-check|stop-hook|test|rule-edit|prompt-edit|config-change|delete-dead-code|other)" "$$f" || { echo "retro-public-check: REFUSED $$b: a line is outside the fixed form" >&2; bad=1; continue; }; c=$$(sed -n 1p "$$f" | cut -d= -f2); v=$$(sed -n 2p "$$f" | cut -d= -f2); r=$$(sed -n 3p "$$f" | cut -d= -f2); o=$$(sed -n 4p "$$f" | cut -d= -f2); x=$$(sed -n 5p "$$f" | cut -d= -f2-); printf "Retro: %%s\n" "$$c" > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/t.$$b"; printf "Week ending: %%s\nClass: %%s\nCount this week: %%s\nLowest fix rung: %%s\nOwning repo: %%s\nSuggested fix kind: %%s\n\nThe evidence is in the private weekly retro report, not on this page.\n" "$$d" "$$c" "$$v" "$$r" "$$o" "$$x" > "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/b.$$b"; rc=0; m=$$(cat "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/t.$$b" "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/b.$$b" | grep -nEif "$$deny") || rc=$$?; if [ "$$rc" -gt 1 ]; then echo "retro-public-check: grep failed on $$b" >&2; exit 1; fi; if [ "$$rc" -eq 0 ]; then echo "retro-public-check: REFUSED $$b: session id, path, quote, time, link, email or secret shape on line(s) $$(printf "%%s\n" "$$m" | cut -d: -f1 | tr "\n" " ")" >&2; bad=1; continue; fi; if grep -Fxq -- "$$c" "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/seen"; then echo "retro: two files for one class, second skipped: $$b"; continue; fi; echo "$$c" >> "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/seen"; echo "$$b" >> "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/queue"; done; if [ "$$bad" -ne 0 ]; then echo "retro-public-check: nothing was filed" >&2; exit 1; fi; echo "retro-public-check: $$n issue file(s) passed"; export GH_CONFIG_DIR="$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/gh" GH_TOKEN=$$(cat "$$RETRO_TOKEN"); while read -r b; do t=$$(cat "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/t.$$b"); if grep -Fxq -- "$$t" "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/known-issues.txt"; then echo "retro: already open, skipped: $$t"; continue; fi; %h/.local/bin/gh issue create -R Nishfleet/fleet-ops --title "$$t" --label retro --body-file "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/b.$$b" < /dev/null; done < "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/queue"'
# The vault copy is the deliverable; a missing one fails the unit even when an
# earlier step died first.
ExecStopPost=/bin/bash -c 'test -s "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/date" || { echo no-deliverable >&2; exit 1; }; d=$$(cat "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/date"); test -s "$$RETRO_VAULT/retro-week-$$d.md" && test "$$RETRO_VAULT/retro-week-$$d.md" -nt "$$RUNTIME_DIRECTORY/date" || { echo no-deliverable >&2; exit 1; }'
TimeoutStartSec=3600
